WASHINGTON, D.C. – Today, U.S. Senator Tammy Baldwin (D-WI) recognized the importance of national marine sanctuaries with a resolution designating October 23, 2023 as National Marine Sanctuary Day. The resolution highlights the role of national marine sanctuaries in increasing access to nature, protecting biodiversity, and boosting economic activity for coastal communities.

“In Wisconsin, the Great Lakes are essential to our way of life. Last year, I was proud to help establish the first national marine sanctuary in the Badger State to ensure that Wisconsinites can enjoy our pristine natural waters for generations to come," said Senator Baldwin. “This resolution will honor the critical role that marine sanctuaries play in preserving our abundant fresh waters, supporting local economies, and driving tourism for coastal communities.”

Senator Baldwin has fought to support national marine sanctuaries, successfully leading the charge to bring a National Marine Sanctuary to Wisconsin in 2021. In October 2013, Senator Baldwin urged NOAA to re-open the public nomination process for marine sanctuaries for the first time in 20 years. After the Administration announced in June 2014 that Americans would be given the opportunity to nominate nationally significant marine and Great Lakes areas as national marine sanctuaries, Wisconsin’s Lake Michigan proposal was submitted and Senator Baldwin called on NOAA to support their efforts. The Wisconsin Shipwreck Coast National Marine Sanctuary was officially designated in 2021.

As a member of the Senate Appropriations Committee, Senator Baldwin has continued to advocate for Wisconsin’s Great Lakes by supporting robust funding for the National Marine Sanctuaries Program and by requesting federal funding for the Wisconsin Shipwreck Coast National Marine Sanctuary Foundation.

The resolution was co-sponsored by Senators Chris Van Hollen (D-MD), Richard Blumenthal (D-CT), Raphael Warnock (D-GA), Peter Welch (D-VT), Patty Murray (D-WA), Brian Schatz (D-HI) and Martin Heinrich (D-NM) and is supported by the National Marine Sanctuary Foundation.

"For over 50 years, national marine sanctuaries have engaged with local partners across the country to conserve and protect some of our nation's most spectacular marine habitats, wildlife, archaeological wonders, and cultural seascapes. Wisconsin Shipwreck Coast National Marine Sanctuary embodies the idea that conserving special places in the ocean and Great Lakes can unite communities and sustain local economies by providing opportunities for people to discover, recreate, and form lifelong connections with these special places," said Russ Green, Superintendent of Wisconsin Shipwreck Coast National Marine Sanctuary. "As the second sanctuary in the Great Lakes, and with more on the horizon, we are grateful for today's recognition and remain firmly committed to a vision of a locally-led, inclusive effort to be better stewards of the waters upon which we all depend."

"Our national marine sanctuaries are partnerships of people, and sources of solutions to the greatest challenges facing our ocean. From the Pacific to the East Coast, the Great Lakes to the Gulf, sanctuaries illuminate history, protect biodiversity, and fight climate change. I thank Senator Baldwin and the co-sponsors for their support of National Marine Sanctuary Day and protecting our waters," said National Marine Sanctuary Foundation President and CEO Joel R. Johnson.

Full text of the Senate Resolution is available here and below:

Designating October 23, 2023, as ‘‘National Marine Sanctuary Day’’.

